{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>By the mirrors of the women who served.</b> These mirrors were of shining copper, very brightly gilded, and it would have been fitting to mention them above together with the women's donation along with the armlets, nose-rings, rings, and כומז; but those ornaments were of gold, and he did not wish to mix the mirrors in with them, for they were of copper, and therefore he mentioned them separately. And there is another reason as well: the donation of gold was entirely mixed into the work of the Tabernacle, but from the mirrors a distinct vessel was made unto itself, namely the laver. And it was for this reason that the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted that Moses our master despised the mirrors because they are made for the evil inclination. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: These are more beloved to Me than everything, for through them the women produced many hosts in Egypt. And they did not interpret thus of the כומז as they interpreted of the mirrors — and Moses our master, peace be upon him, should have despised it even more; but the matter is that there the entire donation was mixed in, whereas here a distinct vessel was made from the mirrors, and therefore it was difficult for Moses until the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: These are more beloved than all. And you already know that the laver made from the mirrors was to bring peace between a man and his wife, for from there they would draw water to give to a woman whose husband had become jealous of her and she had secluded herself." "<b>And the sage</b> R. Abraham, of blessed memory, explained that these women who donated the mirrors were ascetics, removed from the desires of the world, and they served the Blessed Name and gave their mirrors as a donation. And what it says, אשר צבאו פתח אהל מועד — 'who served at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting' — means that among them were many hosts who would come each and every day to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting to pray and to hear; and this is what Onkelos translated: דאתיין לצלאה לתרע משכן זמנא — 'who came to pray at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting.' And the meaning of 'Tent of Meeting' is that the women brought that donation to the Tent of Meeting, which Moses called 'Tent of Meeting,' in the manner of what is written (Exodus 33:7): ״ומשה יקח את האהל וקרא לו אהל מועד״ — 'And Moses took the tent and called it the Tent of Meeting' — for the tent of the Tabernacle had not yet been made."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:40.429097660Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}